### Tested versions

Discovered in v4.5.1.stable.mono.official [f62fdbde1]

### System information

Godot v4.5.1.stable.mono - Windows 10 (build 19045) - Multi-window, 2 monitors - Vulkan (Forward+) - dedicated NVIDIA GeForce GTX 1660 SUPER (NVIDIA; 32.0.15.8129) - Intel(R) Core(TM) i5-9400F CPU @ 2.90GHz (6 threads) - 15.93 GiB memory

### Issue description

The code below demonstrates a minimal reproducible example where Godot's C# build pipeline crashes instead of reporting a compiler error. The override in the child class is invalid and in a standard .NET environment produces a compiler error. In Godot, this results in an unhandled MSBuild failure that cascades into additional build errors rather than surfacing the original compiler diagnostic.

```
using Godot;

public partial class CsCrasher : Node  //<= must inherit from Node
{
	public virtual string Property { get; set; }
}

public partial class CsCrasherChild : CsCrasher
{
	//public override string Property => "Hello world!";
	// ^ this line causes the build pipeline to crash ^
	//note that the property's signature changed from {get;set} to just {get;}
}
```

### Steps to reproduce

1. Create a C# script that inherits from Node or any derived class
2. Declare a non-private virtual property with both `get` and `set`
3. Override this property in a derived class with a `get`ter-only property
4. Attempt to build the project

